Title = {Reverse engineering of BNIP3 identifies a mitochondrial protective
   peptide},
Journal = {NATURE COMMUNICATIONS},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {17},
Number = {1},
Month = {JUN 17},
Abstract = {Recent advances in mitochondrial network dynamic and signalling
   highlight mitochondria as key therapeutic targets across diverse
   diseases. Yet, high drug development failure rates reflect an incomplete
   understanding of upstream molecular regulators of mitochondrial fate.
   Here, we address this gap by reverse engineering of the BH3-only protein
   BNIP3. Structural modelling and sequence-function analyses of its
   N-terminus identify a critical functional domain and amino acid hotspots
   that directly activate BCL-2 executioner proteins, triggering
   mitochondrial cell death. Leveraging these insights, we develop a BNIP3
   antagonist peptide (B-017) that disrupts interactions between BNIP3 and
   BCL-2 executioner proteins, preserving mitochondrial integrity. B-017
   demonstrates target specificity, a favourable safety profile, and robust
   suppression of cell death signalling in human cells. In clinically
   relevant animal models, it reduces tissue damage in the heart, brain,
   and liver. Together, these findings position B-017 as a promising
   therapeutic candidate targeting mitochondrial dysfunction.},
